  "raw": {
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1313770": {
      "title": "Academic Support Coordinator",
      "description": "Academic Support Coordinator. St. Ambrose University. Academic Support Coordinator Mount Mercy University seeks an Academic Support Coordinator of Access & Disability Services (ADS) and Academic Coaching & Enrichment (ACE). This is a full-time, 10.5-month position on the Mount Mercy campus in Cedar Rapids, Iowa. The Academic Support Coordinator works directly with students, faculty and staff and plays a significant role in supporting Access & Disability Services and Academic Coaching Enrichment in ACE. Occasional evening and/or weekend hours may be required. Key Responsibilities Provides in-person, direct support to faculty, students, and administrative staff with consistent and dependable attendance during scheduled work hours. Provide general office functional support to A&DS and ACE staff including but not limited to data entry, filing, processing supply orders and managing invoices. Confidentially manage detailed testing schedules and exam procedures as well as the collection, organization and tracking of testing materials and data. Coordinate alternative testing processes and procedures. Assist with the check-out, setup, and training of assistive technology available to students registered with A&DS. Provide support for the Peer Educator program assisting with scheduling, time-clock processes, and other tasks as requested by ACE staff. Assist with campus communication for A&DS and ACE sponsored events and schedules. Required Qualifications High School diploma At least 3 years of experience in disability services or in higher education, counseling, special education, psychology, or related field. Proficiency in using MS Excel as well as able to effectively use other office productivity software including but not limited to, Microsoft 365, Outlook, Teams, and Adobe. Proven ability to manage sensitive information with discretion and maintain strict confidentiality in accordance with FERPA, ADAA and institutional policies. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor's degree Experience in higher education or a complex organizational environment. Knowledge of employment laws and HR best practices. Benefits: Competitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including medical, dental, vision, and retirement plans. Generous paid time off and holidays. Tuition Remission. Professional development opportunities. Supportive and collaborative work environment. Access to campus amenities, cultural events, and recreational facilities. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University in Cedar Rapids, Iowa, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions within its Catholic higher education network are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit your resume, cover letter, and contact information for three professional references though our online application portal at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1305822": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructors The School of Education at St. Ambrose University seeks qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors and clinical supervisors in its educator preparation programs. By applying, candidates are being considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis depending on programmatic needs and student enrollment. We are seeking candidates with expertise in one or more of the following areas: K-6 Literacy 5-8 Literacy K-12 English as a Second Language (ESL) Early Childhood Education with Inclusive Settings Supervision of clinical and student teaching experiences Adjunct instructors may teach undergraduate students in face-to-face formats during the instructional day on the Davenport campus. Clinical supervisors may support teacher candidates in field-based and student teaching placements in the Quad City area. A master's degree in education or a closely related field is required. Candidates must also possess K-12 teaching experience in relevant educational settings. Preference may be given to applicants with experience supporting teacher candidates, mentoring preservice teachers, or working in diverse and inclusive school environments. At St. Ambrose University, we are committed to preparing reflective, ethical, and student-centered educators who positively impact schools and communities. The university is an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners empowering all members to seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. Interested individuals should apply online at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com a nd submit: A cover letter describing areas of expertise and teaching experience A curriculum vitae or professional resume Contact information for three professional references Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis as instructional and supervision needs arise. EOE"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1318720": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty-Drawing",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty-Drawing. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Faculty-Drawing Mount Mercy University, located in Cedar Rapids, Iowa invites applications to apply for an adjunct faculty position in drawing. The successful candidate will possess some combination of the following with respect to credentials and experience: Have an MFA in fine art or a closely related field, or be actively pursuing an MFA in fine art. Applicants with demonstrated university-level teaching experience will receive special consideration. A strong portfolio of personal drawing work and/or examples of student drawing work Possess competency in foundational drawing techniques including, but not limited to: media proficiency, linework, perspective, value and tone, positive/negative space The individual hired for this position will be expected to embrace the following responsibilities and objectives: Teaching 1 course of AD100 - Drawing, Holding 1 office hour per week. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University in Cedar Rapids, Iowa, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions within its Catholic higher education network are equal opportunity employers. Candidates should apply online at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com by submitting their qualifications for, and interest in, the position, a curriculum vitae (CV) or resume, contact information for three professional references. Please email a portfolio of student work and a portfolio of professional work (PDF or website link). Submissions should be emailed as Microsoft Word or Adobe Acrobat attachments to: HumanResources@sau.edu with the subject line: \"Adjunct Faculty-Drawing.\" To be ensured of full consideration, candidate materials should be received by August 7, 2026. The review of application materials will begin at that time and continue until the position is filled. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332929": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Philosophy, Political Science, Theology",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor - Philosophy, Political Science, Theology. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum - Philosophy, Political Science, Theology The Core Curriculum program and Civics, Ethics, and Catholic Intellectual Tradition division at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for Philosophy, Political Science, and Theology courses in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching one or more of the following courses: PHIL 100. The Examined Life fulfills the Philosophical Foundations Core requirement. This course explores the question, \"What does it mean to live a meaningful life in light of the world we hope for?\", by introducing basic philosophical tools to engage with contemporary and enduring questions such as this. With these tools, students examine the deeper assumptions and the practical consequences of time-honored responses. T hey will learn how to parse, clarify, and better articulate the questions themselves, reflect on possible answers, and defend these with careful, well-constructed arguments that illuminate the various aspects of our lives and what it means to be human. PHIL 146. Humanity, Conflict, and Nonviolence fulfills the Evidence-Based Reasoning: Diverse Human Experience Core requirement. Guided by the question, \" How do experiences of conflict help clarify one's sense of a meaningful life and the common good? \" , the course examines how conflict shapes understandings of purpose, identity, ethics, and community. Students explore conflict across multiple social scales-from struggles with nature and self to interpersonal power dynamics, community dilemmas, and global conflicts. Using evidence-based reasoning, they analyze diverse experiences of conflict and nonviolence across cultures and historical periods while considering the ongoing tension between self-interest and the common good. PHIL 301/THEO 301. Beyond Productivity: Human Thriving in a Culture of Efficiency fulfills the Integrative Faith and Reason Core requirement. This course addresses the essential question: \"In a rapidly changing world driven by technology, how can we thrive?\" Students consider how w e live in a world where rapid technological development has changed the way humans live and relate to each other and their world. Technological innovation promises constant progress. At the same time, the commodification of everything, from water and land to attention and human creativity, leads to experiences of alienation from our bodies, our work, our communities, and the land we inhabit. How do we address this sense of alienation in the midst of human progress? (The course is taught in a specific discipline, depending on the instructor's expertise - e., Philosophy or Theology). PHIL 302/ THEO 302. Thinking Through the Hard Stuff fulfills the Integrative Faith and Reason Core requirement. T his course addresses the question, \"When we face complex real-world problems, how can the big ideas from theology or philosophy help us think through what really matters? This course investigates how philosophical and theological traditions provide resources for addressing complex moral and social problems. Students engage primary texts and significant thinkers to examine how concepts such as justice and the common good shape ethical decision-making. Particular attention is given to distinguishing intuitive reactions from carefully articulated arguments and to assessing the strengths and limits of different frameworks. Students gain practical tools for making sense of our complex world and your place in it. (The course is taught in a specific discipline, depending on the instructor's expertise - e., Philosophy or Theology). PSCI 145. Civil Discourse & Civil Society f ulfills the Communication: Other Core requirement. This course explores the central question: \"How can the practice of civil discourse help us to achieve deeper mutual understanding and healthier social bonds?\" Students will be able to define civility, explain how it respects human dignity and promotes the common good of society, and model civil discourse knowledge, skills, and virtues in a public forum. THEO 100. Understanding Theology and the Common Good fulfills the Theological Foundations Core requirement. This course addresses the following question: \"How can a catholic perspective deepen one's understanding of what it means to create a more just society ?\" Designed as a student's first theology course, this course introduces students to t he ways in which the study of theology and its sources can be applied in a questioning search for deeper understanding in the pursuit of truth and social justice. By engaging the course question, students expand awareness of their own beliefs and those of others about faith perspectives in relation to contemporary issues of justice throughout the world. They also generate collaborative problem-solving skills for assessing theological responses for just action, according to the principle of the common good. THEO 146. Memento Mori: Death & Dying fulfills the Evidence-Based Reasoning: Diverse Human Experience Core requirement. Guided by the question, \"We all die, so why do we interpret death and dying in different ways?\", this introductory course explores how individuals and societies interpret dying, death, and grief. Students will learn basic concepts in the study of death and dying and examine how medical systems, cultural beliefs, religions, and public policies shape end ‑ of ‑ life experiences. The course also compares diverse practices and attitudes across cultures and introduces major ethical questions related to end ‑ of ‑ life care. THEO 300. Saving Nature fulfills the Integrative Faith and Reason Core requirement. This class addresses the complex question of what our responses should be to environmental crises: \"How can we find hope, meaning, and purpose in saving nature?\" Students use experience, academic work, and course resources to understand ecological issues and theological ethics. With this grounding they craft and apply their own environmental ethic to a real environmental issue of their choosing. Throughout this process students are constantly asked to reflect on how our actions to save nature may inspire hope, meaning, and purpose in their lives. Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state employment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332925": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor — Psychology",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor — Psychology. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum - Psychology The Core Curriculum and Psychology programs at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for Psychology courses in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching one or more of the following courses: PSYC 145. Introductory Statistics fulfils the Mathematical Inquiry Core requirement. This introductory statistics course teaches students to organize, visualize, and summarize data, then apply probability concepts to address the enduring question: \" How can we use probability and statistics to tell if something is just random or actually meaningful\"? Students learn to conduct hypothesis tests, interpret data visualizations, and evaluate statistical claims with appropriate skepticism, using technology tools to draw evidence-based conclusions for real-world decision-making. This course requires students to have basic proficiency through high school algebra. PSYC 146. Why Did They Do That? Understanding Human Behavior fulfills the Evidence-Based Reasoning: Diverse Human Experiences Inquiry Core requirement. The course explores the question: \"Why is it important to understand what people do and why they do it\"? Students examine the complexity of human behavior through multiple perspectives that help explain why people think and act as they do. Through evidence-based reasoning and collaborative analysis, students explore diverse human experiences and develop a deeper understanding of, and empathy for others in a complex social Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state employment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience, and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1335228": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Engineering",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor - Engineering. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Faculty, Engineering, Spring 2027 and Future Opportunities St. Ambrose University invites applications for adjunct faculty to teach undergraduate Engineering courses during the Spring 2027 semester at our Davenport, Iowa campus, as well as for consideration for future adjunct teaching opportunities. We are seeking qualified professionals and educators with expertise in mechanical engineering, industrial engineering, manufacturing, engineering design, systems engineering, operations management, materials, mechanics, or related fields. Multiple teaching opportunities are available. All Spring 2027 courses listed below are taught in person during the evening on the St. Ambrose University campus in Davenport, Iowa. Candidates may apply to teach one or more courses based on their qualifications, expertise, and availability. We welcome applicants with expertise in individual course areas and do not expect a single candidate to cover all teaching assignments. Spring 2027 Teaching Opportunities ENGR-296 - Manufacturing Processes Tuesday/Thursday, 6:30-7:45 p.m. ENGR-303 - Strength of Materials Monday/Wednesday, 6:30-7:45 p.m. IE-415 - Systems Integration & Design Monday/Wednesday, 6:30-7:45 p.m. IE-350 - Operations Planning, Scheduling & Control Tuesday/Thursday, 6:30-7:45 p.m. Applicants may review St. Ambrose University course descriptions in the St. Ambrose University Course Catalog to identify courses that align with their academic and professional expertise. Future Adjunct Teaching Opportunities In addition to the Spring 2027 courses listed above, St. Ambrose University welcomes applications from qualified professionals and educators who would like to be considered for future adjunct teaching opportunities in Engineering. These opportunities would require in-person teaching at the Davenport campus. Potential areas of need may include: Mechanical engineering Industrial engineering Manufacturing Engineering design Materials and mechanics Systems engineering Operations management Engineering analysis Other related engineering disciplines Applicants are encouraged to identify additional engineering subject areas or courses they would be interested in teaching in future semesters. Applications may be considered as instructional needs arise. Qualifications A master's degree or higher in engineering or a closely related discipline is preferred. Candidates with significant relevant professional engineering experience and appropriate academic preparation are also encouraged to apply. Previous college-level teaching experience is preferred but not required. Professional experience related to the course subject matter is highly valued. Candidates seeking consideration for Spring 2027 must be able to teach in person at the St. Ambrose University campus in Davenport, Iowa during the scheduled course times. Application Applicants should submit a current curriculum vitae or résumé and a brief cover letter describing their academic and professional background. Applicants should identify the Spring 2027 course or courses for which they would like to be considered, as well as any additional engineering subject areas they may be interested in teaching in future semesters. Course assignments will be based on qualifications, experience, departmental needs, and scheduling availability. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1314265": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor - Philosophy",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor - Philosophy. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor-Philosophy The School of Humanities and Social Sciences in the College of Arts of Sciences at the Mount Mercy University campus, located in Cedar Rapids, IA, seeks qualified applicants to serve as an adjunct instructor in Philosophy for in-person courses in Fall 2026. Adjunct instructors will teach The Examined Life, designed as a student's first philosophy course. A master's degree in philosophy or a closely related field is required. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions within its Catholic higher education network are equal opportunity employers. Interested individuals should apply online at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com and submit a cover letter describing areas of expertise and teaching experience, curriculum vitae or professional resume and contact information for three professional references. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis."
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332992": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor — Kinesiology",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor — Kinesiology. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum - Kinesiology The Core Curriculum and Kinesiology programs at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for Kinesiology courses in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching the following course: KIN 245. The Social Determinants of Health: Local Perspectives fulfills the Local Difference Core requirement. Guided by the question, \" How is feeling good related to your neighborhood?\", t his course is designed around social determinants of health with examples and historical perspectives relevant to SAU campuses (Quad Cities and Cedar Rapids). Students examine multiple forms of \"difference\" related to social determinants of health, a contemporary and enduring concern. Specifically, the course engages in \"local difference,\" focusing on income inequality, discrimination, neighborhood environments, social support, and the migrant experience, with contemporary and historical examples. Students learn to identify the value of diverse identities and perspectives and articulate the role of race, ethnicity, age, sex and gender as social determinants of health. Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state employment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332927": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor — Sociology",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor — Sociology. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum - Sociology The Core Curriculum and Sociology programs at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for Sociology courses in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching one or more of the following courses: SOC 146. Memento Mori: Death & Dying fulfills the Evidence-Based Reasoning: Diverse Human Experience Core requirement. Guided by the question, \"We all die, so why do we interpret death and dying in different ways?\", this introductory course explores how individuals and societies interpret dying, death, and grief. Students will learn basic concepts in the study of death and dying and examine how medical systems, cultural beliefs, religions, and public policies shape end ‑ of ‑ life experiences. The course also compares diverse practices and attitudes across cultures and introduces major ethical questions related to end ‑ of ‑ life care. SOC 245. The Social Determinants of Health: Local Perspectives fulfills the Local Difference Core requirement. Guided by the question, \" How is feeling good related to your neighborhood?\", t his course is designed around social determinants of health with examples and historical perspectives relevant to SAU campuses (Quad Cities and Cedar Rapids). Students examine multiple forms of \"difference\" related to social determinants of health, a contemporary and enduring concern. Specifically, the course engages in \"local difference,\" focusing on income inequality, discrimination, neighborhood environments, social support, and the migrant experience, with contemporary and historical examples. Students learn to identify the value of diverse identities and perspectives and articulate the role of race, ethnicity, age, sex and gender as social determinants of health. SOC 246. Global Impacts of Immigration fulfills the Global Difference Core requirement. Guided by the question, \"How does the global impact of immigration expand and transform our understanding of the value of difference, diversity, and socio-cultural identity?\", this course examines immigration in global contexts and explores how historical and contemporary forces shape personal, collective, and institutional identities. The course examines narratives of immigration across literature, media, case studies, and personal testimonies, fostering intercultural competency and critical reflection on socio-cultural diversity. Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state employment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332978": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor — Statistics",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor — Statistics. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum - Statistics The Core Curriculum and Mathematics programs at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for a Statistics course in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching the following course: STAT 145. Introductory Statistics fulfills the Mathematical Inquiry Core requirement. This introductory statistics course teaches students to organize, visualize, and summarize data, then apply probability concepts to address the enduring question: \" How can we use probability and statistics to tell if something is just random or actually meaningful\"? Students learn to conduct hypothesis tests, interpret data visualizations, and evaluate statistical claims with appropriate skepticism, using technology tools to draw evidence-based conclusions for real-world decision-making. This course requires students to have basic proficiency through high school algebra. Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state employment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332936": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor —Art and Design, Music, Theatre",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor —Art and Design, Music, Theatre. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum -Art and Design, Music, Theatre The Core Curriculum program and Fine and Performing Arts division at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for Art and Design, Music, and Theatre courses in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching one or more of the following courses: AD 145/THTR 145. Creative Expression fulfills the Creative Expression Core requirement. This course engages the question, \"How does engaging in the creative process enrich our understanding of ourselves and others?\", by focusing on exploring artistic techniques through the creative process. Students engage in both individual and collaborative processes to craft original creative works with an emphasis on integrating feedback from professors and peers. The course culminates in students showcasing their work to an audience through exhibition, publication, and/or live performance. (The course is taught in a specific discipline, depending on the instructor's expertise - e., Art and Design, or Theatre). MUS 145. Creating Music in the 21st Century fulfills the Creative Expression Core requirement. This course engages the question, \"How does the music making process influence the way we express ourselves?\", by exploring how the music-making process shapes the way people express ideas, emotions, and perspectives. Students learn foundational concepts of musical elements, sound, and digital music production while creating original compositions. Through listening, analysis, creative projects, and reflection, students examine how musical choices communicate meaning and how music both reflects and influences cultural and social contexts. No prior composition or production experience is required for students who enroll in this course. Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state emp loyment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332987": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor —Astronomy, Physics",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor —Astronomy, Physics. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum -Astronomy, Physics The Core Curriculum, Astronomy, and Physics programs at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for Astronomy and Physics courses in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching one or more of the following courses: ASTR 145. Science in Our Lives fulfills the Evidence-Based Reasoning: Natural World Core requirement. This course addresses the central question: \"How are our lives shaped by scientific development?\" Students d iscover the powerful ways that science shapes our lives in this course, by exploring the significant impacts of scientific developments on our daily routine, our community, and the global stage. Through a variety of activities, including a final project focused on \"Science in Our Lives,\" students critically evaluate a scientific development and its future outcomes. PHYS 146. Thinking Like a Scientist fulfills the Evidence-Based Reasoning: Natural World Core requirement. This course explores the guiding question: \"How can thinking like a scientist help me understand the world around me?\" The course introduces students to scientific thinking through the exploration of real-world phenomena. Students ask questions, investigate scientific ideas, analyze evidence, and communicate findings. Through hands-on investigations and analysis of scientific data, students learn how scientific reasoning can be used to understand and explain the world around them. Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state employment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332931": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor —Communication",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor —Communication. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum -Communication The Core Curriculum and Communication programs at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for Communication courses in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching the following course: COMM 145. Critical Conversations: Finding Your Voice and Building Relationships fulfills the Communication: Other Core requirement. The course explores the guiding question: \"How can communication empower individuals to find their voice and build stronger relationships?\" The course empowers students to examine their role in communication across personal and professional contexts. Students identify how self-identity shapes communication, develop strategies for building stronger relationships, and analyze audience needs to craft compelling messages. By becoming more effective communicators, students gain a competitive advantage in their careers, form more meaningful personal connections, and develop the confidence to advocate for causes they value-essential skills for success in today's multicultural and digital world. Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state employment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332989": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor —English, History",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor —English, History. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum -English, History The Core Curriculum program and Literature, Languages, and Culture division at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for English and History courses in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching one or more of the following courses: ENGL 101. English Composition fulfills the Written Expression Core This course addresses the challenging question: \"How do stories shape lives, communities, and societies\"? This course teaches students to write effectively in college and beyond. Students learn to better communicate their ideas, research, and analysis in written form. ENGL 145. Creative Expression fulfills the Creative Expression Core requirement. This course engages the question, \"How does engaging in the creative process enrich our understanding of ourselves and others?\", by focusing on exploring artistic techniques through the creative writing process. Students engage in both individual and collaborative processes to craft original creative works with an emphasis on integrating feedback from professors and peers. The course culminates in students showcasing their work to an audience through exhibition, publication, and/or live performance. Humanity, Conflict, and Nonviolence fulfills the Evidence-Based Reasoning: Diverse Human Experience Core requirement. Guided by the question, \" How do experiences of conflict help clarify one's sense of a meaningful life and the common good? \" , the course examines how conflict shapes understandings of purpose, identity, ethics, and community. Students explore conflict across multiple social scales-from struggles with nature and self to interpersonal power dynamics, community dilemmas, and global conflicts. Using evidence-based reasoning, they analyze diverse experiences of conflict and nonviolence across cultures and historical periods while considering the ongoing tension between self-interest and the common good. HIST 145. Reacting to the Past fulfills the Evidence-Based Reasoning: Historical Perspectives Core requirement. This course addresses the following question: \"How does developing an historical consciousness allow us to understand and navigate the complexities of the contemporary world\" This course introduces students to the basic skills of historical analysis and understanding. These skills aare used in playing a sequence of carefully designed role-playing games that allow students to adopt diverse perspectives and explore primary and secondary sources to better understand those past time periods. The games help the students explore the past, but with a careful eye on how this knowledge impacts the present and future. Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state employment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332969": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor —Global Studies",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor —Global Studies. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum -Global Studies The Core Curriculum and Global Studies programs at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for Global Studies courses in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching the following course: GST 246. Global Impacts of Immigration fulfills the Global Difference Core requirement. Guided by the question, \"How does the global impact of immigration expand and transform our understanding of the value of difference, diversity, and socio-cultural identity?\", this course examines immigration in global contexts and explores how historical and contemporary forces shape personal, collective, and institutional identities. The course examines narratives of immigration across literature, media, case studies, and personal testimonies, fostering intercultural competency and critical reflection on socio-cultural diversity. Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state employment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332933": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor—Biology, Chemistry",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor—Biology, Chemistry. St. Ambrose University. Adjunct Instructor: Core Curriculum -Biology, Chemistry The Core Curriculum and Biology and Chemistry programs at St. Ambrose University (Davenport, Iowa), along with its affiliate, Mount Mercy University (Cedar Rapids, Iowa) seek qualified applicants to serve as adjunct instructors for Biology and Chemistry courses in the Core Curriculum. By applying, candidates will be considered for inclusion in an adjunct faculty pool. Positions are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on programmatic needs. Course Details and Format Specific course assignments and formats, including online and face-to-face instruction, vary by semester based on teaching needs. Openings develop on a periodic basis. We are seeking candidates with expertise and interest in teaching one or more of the following courses: BIOL 145/CHEM 145. Science in Our Lives fulfills the Evidence-Based Reasoning: Natural World Core requirement. This course addresses the central question: \"How are our lives shaped by scientific development?\" Students d iscover the powerful ways that science shapes our lives in this course, by exploring the significant impacts of scientific developments on our daily routine, our community, and the global stage. Through a variety of activities, including a final project focused on \"Science in Our Lives,\" students critically evaluate a scientific development and its future outcomes. (The course is taught in a specific discipline, depending on the instructor's expertise - e., Biology or Chemistry). BIOL 146/CHEM 146. Thinking Like a Scientist fulfills the Evidence-Based Reasoning: Natural World Core requirement. This course explores the guiding question: \"How can thinking like a scientist help me understand the world around me?\" The course introduces students to scientific thinking through the exploration of real-world phenomena. Students ask questions, investigate scientific ideas, analyze evidence, and communicate findings. Through hands-on investigations and analysis of scientific data, students learn how scientific reasoning can be used to understand and explain the world around them. (The course is taught in a specific discipline, depending on the instructor's expertise - e., Biology or Chemistry). Qualifications Successful candidates must hold a master's degree or have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in the specific subject area or a closely related field. Preference may be given to applicants with prior teaching experience. Due to state employment regulations, candidates must reside in one of the following authorized states to be considered for online adjunct teaching assignments: AK, AZ, AR, CO, FL, GA, IL, IN, IA, LA, MI, MN, MO, NE, NV, ND, SD, TX, VA, WV, or WI. Candidates who reside outside of these states may not be eligible for appointment. Our Mission St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice and mercy, and lead lives of service. The Core Curriculum seeks to advance this mission by preparing students with the knowledge, skills, and ethical grounding to work courageously to create a just future. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply Interested individuals should apply online at: https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com Please submit the following materials: A cover letter describing your areas of expertise and teaching experience and indicating your willingness and/or preference to teach in face-to-face (in Davenport or Cedar Rapids) or online modalities . A curriculum vitae or professional résumé. Contact information for three professional references. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1332923": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor – Tenure Track - MSW",
      "description": "Assistant Professor – Tenure Track - MSW. St. Ambrose University. M aster of Social Work Assistant Professor St. Ambrose University in Davenport, Iowa, invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position in Social Work at the rank of Assistant Professor to start as soon as January 1 st , 2027, and no later than July 1 st , 2027. This individual hired for this position will have primary teaching responsibilities in the Master of Social Work Program. The faculty member holding this position will: Teach research methods courses, as well as other courses across the social work curriculum Teach in a variety of course delivery systems, i.e., in-person, online synchronous and asynchronous, and hybrid. Serve as a Practicum Liaison for social work students Be an active advisor to students Participate and provide leadership on university committees Engage in scholarly activity One of eight disciplines within the College of Health and Human Services, the MSW program offers a supportive, collaborative environment with a strong focus on teaching, as well as support for scholarship. Multiple interprofessional learning events and numerous outreach services enhance the student experience. Additionally, the program and college value self-directed community involvement, inter-professional collaboration, and active professional development for faculty and staff. This MSW Program has a single concentration in Empowerment Social Work Practice. Program options include an in-person full-time MSW program, part-time online MSW program, and in-person and online Advanced Standing MSW programs. The position requires an MSW from a CSWE-accredited program and a doctorate in social work or related field. Applications from ABDs will be considered. A minimum of two years of post-MSW experience is required, and evidence of effective teaching is essential. Salary will be commensurate with experience. People from under-represented populations are encouraged to apply. Priority will be given to applicants showing a strong history of teaching and practice experience with vulnerable populations. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners empowering all their members to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. St. Ambrose is an equal opportunity employer. You can learn more about St. Ambrose at www.sau.edu. Interested individuals should apply online at https://Stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com by submitting a cover letter addressing their knowledge and experience, a vitae , a statement of their teaching philosophy, and contact information for three professional references. Applications received by October 15, 2026, will be ensured of consideration and applications will continue to be reviewed until the position is filled. EOE. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1334060": {
      "title": "Assistant Registrar – Student Services",
      "description": "Assistant Registrar – Student Services. St. Ambrose University. Assistant Registrar - Student Services This entry-level position coordinates with academic departments to develop course schedules that align student demand with faculty availability while managing classroom assignments. Additional responsibilities include monitoring and responding to office email communications to provide personalized student support and timely customer service, as well as processing enrollment verifications. Other duties may be assigned as needed. Benefits: Competitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including medical, dental, vision, and retirement plans. Generous paid time off and holidays. Tuition Remission. Professional development opportunities. Supportive and collaborative work environment. Access to campus amenities, cultural events, and recreational facilities. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses are an inclusive, Catholic and diocesan community of learners empowering all members of that community to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit your resume, cover letter, and contact information for three professional references though our online application portal at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. EOE #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1322321": {
      "title": "Assistant Registrar- Compliance &amp; Data",
      "description": "Assistant Registrar- Compliance &amp; Data. St. Ambrose University. Assistant Registrar for Compliance & Data Mount Mercy University is seeking a detail-oriented professional to support student data integrity, regulatory compliance, and institutional reporting within the Registrar's Office. Key Responsibilities: Coordinate enrollment reporting to the National Student Clearinghouse. Collect, validate, and report data for federal, state, and institutional requirements, including IPEDS. Conduct audits of student information systems and resolve data discrepancies. Support compliance initiatives and institutional data tracking. Respond to internal data requests and prepare reports for university stakeholders. Assist with graduation audits, degree verification, and student record maintenance. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree or equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience with databases, student information systems, or data management. Strong analytical, organizational, and communication skills. Knowledge of higher education regulations, reporting requirements, and data privacy preferred. Ability to manage multiple priorities while maintaining accuracy and confidentiality. Benefits: Competitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including medical, dental, vision, and retirement plans. Generous paid time off and holidays. Tuition Remission. Professional development opportunities. Supportive and collaborative work environment. Access to campus amenities, cultural events, and recreational facilities. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University in Cedar Rapids, Iowa, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions within its Catholic higher education network are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit your resume, cover letter, and contact information for three professional references through our online application portal at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1309721": {
      "title": "Assistant Volleyball Coach",
      "description": "Assistant Volleyball Coach. St. Ambrose University. Assistant Men's Volleyball Coach Are you passionate about coaching and helping student-athletes achieve their full potential both on and off the court? St. Ambrose University is seeking a dynamic individual to join our Athletics Department in this 10-month position as an Assistant Men's Volleyball Coach. The successful candidate will have coaching responsibilities in volleyball and assist in the recruitment of the men's volleyball program. Key Responsibilities: Participate in coaching during practices and games, providing constructive feedback and instruction. Work closely with student-athletes to develop skills, improve performance, and foster team unity. Contribute to strategy development for both individual players and team play, ensuring consistent progression. Assist the Head Coach in the day-to-day operations of the Men's Volleyball program, including but not limited to: practice planning, game preparation, recruiting, and team management. Assist in recruiting qualified student-athletes to join the volleyball program. Collaborate with the athletic department to ensure the overall well-being and academic success of student-athletes. Ensure compliance with NAIA, conference, and institutional rules and regulations. Support team travel logistics, as needed, including organizing transportation and accommodations for away games. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree (required). Previous experience as a volleyball coach (preferably at the collegiate level). Strong understanding of volleyball fundamentals, strategy, and player development. Excellent communication, leadership, and interpersonal skills. Ability to work effectively in a team-oriented environment. Knowledge of NAIA rules and regulations (preferred). A commitment to the academic and athletic success of student-athletes. Benefits: 10-month position, August through May. Salary is $20,000 plus housing. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses are an inclusive, Catholic and diocesan community of learners empowering all their members to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. How to Apply: Join a university with a commitment to excellence in both academics and athletics. To apply please submit your resume, cover letter, and contact information for three professional references though our online application portal at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com . Position is open until filled. St. Ambrose University is an equal opportunity employer. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1334067": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Academic Coaching and Enrichment",
      "description": "Associate Director of Academic Coaching and Enrichment. St. Ambrose University. Associate Director of Academic Coaching and Enrichment Position Summary St. Ambrose University is currently accepting applications for an Associate Director of Academic Coaching & Enrichment within our Academic Coaching & Enrichment Center (ACE) at our St. Ambrose Davenport campus. This is a full-time exempt position. Primary Responsibilities Provide operational leadership for the Academic Coaching & Enrichment (ACE) Center on the assigned campus. Ensure a positive, welcoming environment and experience for all students, faculty, staff, and guests who visit the Academic Coaching & Enrichment Center (ACE Center) and the university campus. Support all aspects of the university academic support programs and resources. Provide supervision and evaluation of assigned staff on the assigned campus. Responsible for managing day-to-day operations and academic retention initiatives to support student progression toward degree completion. Coordinate academic coaching services which include training and supporting academic coaches as well as providing direct academic coaching services to assigned students. Oversee the recruitment, hiring, training, and supervision of student employees (Graduate Assistants, content tutors, peer academic coaches, writing tutors) within the Academic Coaching & Enrichment (ACE) Center. Coordinate and manage the Peer Educator/Student Employee program, including hiring, training, scheduling and supervision of student employees. This includes goal setting, time-clock processes, managing program schedules, and evaluation processes. Maintain high-quality customer service and foster collaborative partnerships among staff, faculty, and university leadership to support persistence and retention efforts. Work in partnership with faculty and university leadership to develop efficient processes to identify students in need of academic support and connect identified students with available resources. Collaborate with colleagues across the university network to strengthen and align academic outreach programs with emerging trends and data-based best practices for academic support programs in higher education. Teach assigned sections of academic development courses and assist in the development and revision of academic development courses according to university processes and procedures. Coordinate and lead day-to-day operations of available summer BRIDGE programs on the assigned campus. Manage the collection, tracking, storage, and reporting of confidential data pertaining to academic support programs according to campus data integrity policy, FERPA, and End User compliance. Maintain oversight of academic center software management programs on assigned campus to include training staff, collection of data, and identifying outreach opportunities based on early alert data. Provide outreach and marketing to the campus community via email, social media posts, campus communication resources, and events to increase engagement in academic support and enrichment programs. Represent ACE and academic support programs to current and prospective students, faculty, staff, and visitors at campus events including campus visit days, registration days, and other outreach opportunities. Occasional evening and/or weekend hours are required. General Classification Requirements Master's degree in related field. Priority for candidates with 3-5 years' experience in higher education in the area(s) of academic coaching, retention, student affairs, academic support, or related areas. Specific Requirements Commitment to student success, retention, and inclusive educational practices. Demonstrate excellent communication and organizational skills. Ability to create rapport and positive academic coaching relationships with identified students to encourage and increase participation in academic support programs and events. Relate positively and respectfully to all students, faculty, staff, and visitors from a variety of backgrounds. Effectively use Microsoft 365, Outlook, Excel, and other office productivity software i.e. Adobe and Canva. Knowledge of current legislation regarding FERPA and ADAA, with a commitment to assuring a high level of confidentiality and professionalism. Work both independently and collaboratively with department team members in a student-centered fast-paced university academic center. Benefits: Competitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including medical, dental, vision, and retirement plans. Generous paid time off and holidays. Tuition Remission. Professional development opportunities. Supportive and collaborative work environment. Access to campus amenities, cultural events, and recreational facilities. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses are an inclusive, Catholic and diocesan community of learners empowering all members of that community to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit your resume, cover letter, and contact information for three professional references though our online application portal at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. EOE #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1334063": {
      "title": "CDL Bus Driver - Athletics",
      "description": "CDL Bus Driver - Athletics. St. Ambrose University. PRIMARY FUNCTION: The Athletic Team Driver will be responsible for safely transporting the assigned athletic team to and from athletic event via the assigned team vehicle. The primary modes of transportation are the institutional 25 and 35 passenger buses, or occasionally a 15 passenger van. A class \"B\" CDL driver's license with a passenger endorsement is required to operate the buses. Previous bus driving experience is strongly preferred. (Part-Time - 10 Months - August - May) ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS 1. Safely drive the team vehicle in a timely manner to and from the assigned event. 2. Ability to assess and correct minor maintenance issues that may arise on a trip (wiper blades, jump start a battery, change a flat tire, etc.). 3. Ability to read and follow navigation equipment. 4. Ability to safely drive in inclement weather as necessary (snow, rain, etc.). 5. Ability to safely drive earlier in the morning or later at night as necessary. 6. Attendance as scheduled. 7. Punctuality 8. Obey traffic laws. 9. Ensure the safety of all passengers. 10. Assist passengers with getting on and off the bus as needed. 11. Perform inspections of the bus before and after each trip. 12. Attend vehicle orientation / safety meetings as required. 13. Record and report mileage after each trip. *ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: To perform this job successfully, the employee must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. A qualified individual is one who, with or without reasonable accommodations, can perform the essential functions of the position . GENERAL CLASSIFICATION REQUIREMENTS High School graduate or GED and 2 years' experience as a driver; previous bus driving experience preferred SPECIFIC REQUIREMENTS (Necessary to fill this position) Requirements: Class \"B\" CDL with Passenger Endorsement is Required Valid driver license Must be at least 21 years of age Medical Certificate (in accordance with the Federal Motor Carrier Safety Regulations) Must have a safe driving record with a good knowledge of Iowa Driver's Manual and DOT's A Motor Carrier's Guide to Improving Highway Safety Ability to communicate effectively and exercise good human relations skills in establishing and maintaining effective working relations with the university community Compliance with all applicable DOT regulations, including submission to drug and alcohol testing A background check will be required of the successful applicant Must be willing and available for evening and weekend work"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1266046": {
      "title": "Nursing Clinical Adjunct Faculty",
      "description": "Nursing Clinical Adjunct Faculty. St. Ambrose University. The Mount Mercy Department of Nursing invites qualified applicants to apply for an adjunct clinical nursing instructor position for the Spring Semester, 2026. Adjunct clinical instructors serve semester to semester based on student enrollment. Candidates are required to have a minimum of two years of clinical experience, a Baccalaureate degree in Nursing and an unencumbered RN license in the state of Iowa. Preference for dual licensure with unencumbered RN licenses in the states of Iowa and Illinois. Prior teaching experience preferred. At St. Ambrose University and the Mount Mercy campus we are more than an institution of higher learning; we are a beacon for those seeking to impact the world significantly. Guided by our foundation beliefs and values, our mission and vision reflect our dedication to fostering an inclusive, ethical, and dynamic learning environment. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit a cover letter and curriculum vitae through our online application portal at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. EOE"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1284705": {
      "title": "Nursing Clinical Adjunct Instructor - Quad Cities Area",
      "description": "Nursing Clinical Adjunct Instructor - Quad Cities Area. St. Ambrose University. The St. Ambrose University Department of Nursing is seeking candidates to join its pool of clinical adjunct faculty to teach nursing students in clinical settings. By applying, candidates are being considered for inclusion in a pool of instructors. Positions are available each semester and are filled on a semester-by-semester basis, depending on student enrollment needs. Applications will be reviewed as needs arise. Candidates must have a Bachelor of Science in Nursing (BSN) and at least two years of nursing experience in a clinical setting. We are seeking candidates with a current registered nurse license (either single-state or multistate) in the Quad Cities area. We have ongoing needs for clinical instructors in various settings. At St. Ambrose University, we are more than an institution of higher learning; we are a beacon for those seeking to impact the world significantly. Guided by our foundation beliefs and values, our mission and vision reflect our dedication to fostering an inclusive, ethical, and dynamic learning environment. Interested individuals should apply on-line at https://Stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com and upload a resume, and contact information for three professional references . EOE For additional information about the position, contact nursing@sau.edu. More information about the St. Ambrose University Department of Nursing can be found on our website at www.sau.edu/nursing."
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1331407": {
      "title": "Program Director – Occupational Therapy",
      "description": "Program Director – Occupational Therapy. St. Ambrose University. Program Director, Occupational Therapy St. Ambrose University invites applications for a full-time Program Director position in the Occupational Therapy Department. The Program Director provides academic and administrative leadership for the occupational therapy program and advances an inclusive, student-centered learning community grounded in professional excellence, ethical practice, service, and social responsibility. Located in Davenport, Iowa, St. Ambrose University is an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. The University empowers all Ambrosians to act courageously, seek wisdom through faith and reason, work for justice, and lead lives of service. The Occupational Therapy Program Director will model and advance these commitments through collaborative leadership, high-quality occupational therapy education, meaningful community engagement, and preparation of graduates who support the health, participation, dignity, and well-being of diverse individuals and communities. Position Summary The Program Director is the academic and administrative leader of the Occupational Therapy program. The successful candidate will provide leadership for program planning, curriculum implementation and evaluation, faculty development, student success, accreditation compliance, budget oversight, community and clinical partnerships, and continuous quality improvement. In accordance with ACOTE Standard A.2.1, the Program Director must be a full-time faculty member appointed to the occupational therapy degree-level program. The Program Director is responsible for management and administration of the program, including planning, evaluation, budgeting, faculty and staff selection, maintenance of accreditation, and strategies for professional development. Essential Responsibilities The Program Director will: Provide strategic, academic, and operational leadership for the Occupational Therapy program in alignment with University, college, department, and professional accreditation expectations. Ensure ongoing compliance with all applicable Accreditation Council for Occupational Therapy Education (ACOTE) standards, policies, reporting requirements, and accreditation timelines. Lead program assessment, curriculum review, outcomes evaluation, strategic planning, and continuous quality-improvement initiatives using data-informed and evidence-based processes. Oversee the design, implementation, evaluation, and revision of an inclusive curriculum that prepares graduates for ethical, entry-level occupational therapy practice. Manage program operations, including planning, budgeting, resource allocation, facilities and equipment needs, faculty and staff selection, and administrative processes. Recruit, develop, mentor, evaluate, and retain qualified faculty, adjunct faculty, and staff; promote a collegial culture of professional growth, scholarly engagement, teaching excellence, and shared governance. Support student recruitment, admission, progression, retention, advising, professional development, and timely completion, while fostering a learning environment characterized by belonging, accountability, compassion, and high expectations. Collaborate with academic fieldwork coordinators, capstone personnel as applicable, clinical and community partners, alumni, employers, and professional organizations to maintain strong educational and practice partnerships. Teach in the occupational therapy curriculum in areas consistent with professional expertise and program needs. Promote faculty and student scholarship, service, leadership, advocacy, and engagement with local, regional, and national occupational therapy communities. Represent the program and University professionally within the community, University committees, accreditation activities, recruitment events, professional organizations, and external partnerships. Required Qualifications Consistent with ACOTE Standard A.2.1, applicants must demonstrate the following qualifications: Initial certification as an occupational therapist. Current Iowa occupational therapy license or eligibility for licensure in Iowa. A doctoral degree awarded by an institution accredited by a U.S. Department of Education-recognized institutional accrediting agency. A minimum of eight years of documented experience in the field of occupational therapy. Documented experience that includes clinical occupational therapy practice, administrative experience, scholarship, understanding of the role of the occupational therapy assistant, and faculty experience with teaching responsibilities appropriate to the degree level offered by the program. Demonstrated ability to manage and administer an occupational therapy educational program, including planning, evaluation, budgeting, faculty and staff selection, accreditation maintenance, and professional-development initiatives. Demonstrated knowledge of ACOTE accreditation standards, policies, and reporting processes. 4 years of experience as a faculty member with evidence of effective teaching at the postbaccalaureate level, mentoring, communication, collaborative leadership, and commitment to student success. Commitment to ethical practice, inclusion, community engagement, and the values of occupational therapy. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates with one or more of the following: Prior experience as an occupational therapy program director, department chair, academic fieldwork coordinator, capstone coordinator, or comparable academic leadership role. Experience leading ACOTE self-studies, accreditation reporting, substantive-change processes, program evaluation, or accreditation-site-visit preparation. Record of scholarship, professional presentations, grant activity, community-engaged work, or leadership within AOTA, IOTA, state associations, or other professional organizations. Experience with curriculum development, competency-based assessment, student retention initiatives, simulation, interprofessional education, or innovative instructional design. Demonstrated success building partnerships with healthcare systems, schools, community organizations, and occupational therapy practice settings. Application Materials Applicants should submit: A letter of application addressing qualifications and interest in the position Curriculum vitae Contact information for three professional references St. Ambrose University is committed to creating and sustaining an inclusive educational community that welcomes individuals of varied beliefs, backgrounds, identities, and experiences. The University encourages applications from qualified candidates who will contribute to its Catholic, diocesan mission and its commitment to courage, wisdom, justice, and service. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1320085": {
      "title": "Senior Systems &amp; Network Administrator",
      "description": "Senior Systems &amp; Network Administrator. St. Ambrose University. Senior Systems & Network Administrator St. Ambrose University is seeking a Senior Systems & Network Administrator at either our St. Ambrose campus in Davenport, IA or our Mount Mercy campus in Cedar Rapids, IA to manage and support the technology infrastructure that keeps our campuses connected. This role is responsible for maintaining secure, reliable server and network environments while helping advance technology solutions that support students, faculty, and staff. Key Responsibilities Manage physical and virtual servers, storage systems, backups, and Windows Server environments. Administer and support Cisco and Aruba network infrastructure, including wired and wireless networks, VLANs, firewalls, VPNs, and IP management. Monitor system and network performance, security, and reliability. Provide Tier III technical support and troubleshoot complex infrastructure issues. Maintain documentation, policies, and operational procedures. Evaluate new technologies and recommend infrastructure improvements. Participate in an on-call rotation for occasional after-hours support. Qualifications Two or more years of systems and/or network administration experience. Experience with Cisco and/or Aruba networking technologies. Knowledge of virtualization, Microsoft 365, Windows Server, and enterprise infrastructure tools. Strong troubleshooting and problem-solving skills. Excellent communication, organizational, and customer service skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities in a collaborative environment. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Information Technology, Computer Science, or a related field. Certifications such as CCNA, VCTA, Microsoft 365 Fundamentals, or CompTIA A+. Experience working in higher education Benefits: Competitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including medical, dental, vision, and retirement plans. Generous paid time off and holidays. Tuition Remission. Professional development opportunities. Supportive and collaborative work environment. Access to campus amenities, cultural events, and recreational facilities. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University in Cedar Rapids, Iowa, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions within its Catholic higher education network are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit your resume, cover letter, and contact information for three professional references though our online application portal at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1327343": {
      "title": "Student Financial Services Representative",
      "description": "Student Financial Services Representative. St. Ambrose University. Student Financial Services Representative St. Ambrose University is hiring a Student Financial Services Representative to support a one‑stop, student-centered service model across Financial Aid and Student Accounts. This is a frontline role assisting students and families with financial aid questions, billing/payment support, and policy guidance by phone, email, and in person. Key Duties Provide high-quality customer service to students/families (phone, email, in person) Answer questions related to financial aid, billing, payments, and university policies Assist with FAFSA, verification, and related financial aid documentation Review student accounts, explain charges, and help resolve account issues/restrictions Process transactions (payments, payment plans) and support daily office operations Educate students on policies/processes such as: refunds/direct deposit, 1098‑T, student payroll, housing deposits billing systems (e.g., TouchNet) Use judgment to resolve routine issues and escalate complex cases appropriately Maintain accuracy and confidentiality; ensure compliance with FERPA and applicable regulations Support reporting, data entry, and occasional project/process improvement work Perform opening/closing cash procedures following internal controls Cross-train and assist across Student Financial Services as needed Required Qualifications Associate's degree 3+ years customer service experience (higher education preferred) Strong communication skills (written/verbal) and professionalism under pressure Conflict resolution skills and commitment to serving diverse populations Strong attention to detail; ability to multitask in a fast-paced environment Proficiency in Microsoft Office (Excel, Word) and web-based systems Preferred Experience in financial aid, student accounts, registrar, or student services setting Familiarity with TouchNet or similar billing/payment platforms Work Environment Fast-paced, high-volume, student-facing, service-oriented office environment Occasional schedule flexibility to meet operational needs Benefits: Competitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including medical, dental, vision, and retirement plans. Generous paid time off and holidays. Tuition Remission. Professional development opportunities. Supportive and collaborative work environment. Access to campus amenities, cultural events, and recreational facilities. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses are an inclusive, Catholic and diocesan community of learners empowering all members of that community to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit your resume, cover letter, and contact information for three professional references though our online application portal at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. EOE"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1322422": {
      "title": "Writing Support Specialist",
      "description": "Writing Support Specialist. St. Ambrose University. Writing Support Specialist (Part-Time) Mount Mercy University is seeking a part-time Writing Support Specialist to join the Academic Coaching & Enrichment Center (ACE). This position plays a vital role in supporting undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students by providing writing assistance, literacy development, and academic success programming across the university network. Schedule: 25 hours per week during the fall and spring semesters. This position is based in Cedar Rapids with remote support provided to other campuses. Flexible daytime and evening availability is required. Key Responsibilities Develop and lead academic writing support initiatives based on current trends and best practices. Provide individual writing and English language literacy support to undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral students through in-person and virtual appointments. Teach 1-2 sections of academic writing support courses (1-credit) as assigned. Collaborate with faculty to deliver classroom presentations, workshops, and writing resources. Create writing workshops, citation guides, instructional materials, and online resources for students and faculty. Assist with the hiring, training, and supervision of Peer Educator Writing Tutors across campuses. Provide outreach and training to writing tutors on the St. Ambrose University Davenport campus. Support a welcoming and positive learning environment for students, visitors, and prospective students. Represent academic support programs at campus events, registration days, visit days, and outreach activities. Demonstrate effective communication, organization, and time management skills. Qualifications Required Bachelor's degree. Strong background in writing instruction, writing consultation, or literacy development. Experience supporting diverse learners, including English language learners and students requiring academic support. Ability to collaborate effectively with faculty and campus partners. Commitment to student success, retention, and inclusive educational practices. Preferred Master's degree in English, Writing, Education, Student Affairs, or a related field. Experience in higher education academic support services, writing instruction, tutoring programs, or student success initiatives. Experience coordinating tutoring programs and supervising student employees. Benefits Competitive salary commensurate with experience. Comprehensive benefits package, including medical, dental, vision, and retirement plans. Generous paid time off and holidays. Tuition Remission. Professional development opportunities. Supportive and collaborative work environment. Access to campus amenities, cultural events, and recreational facilities. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University in Cedar Rapids, Iowa, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions within its Catholic higher education network are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit your resume, cover letter, and contact information for three professional references though our online application portal at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com . Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. #HEJ"
    },
    "https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com/jobs/1335969": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant for Occupational Therapy",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant for Occupational Therapy. St. Ambrose University. Administrative Assistant St. Ambrose University is seeking a full-time Administrative Assistant to join the Occupational Therapy Department. This position plays an important role in supporting the department's academic, clinical education, and experiential learning activities. The Administrative Assistant coordinates a variety of essential departmental processes, including course support, clinical contracts and affiliation agreements, and documentation of university and student requirements related to clinical education and fieldwork compliance. The position works independently with minimal supervision and serves as a key point of contact for students, faculty, clinical partners, and others through in-person, phone, and email communication. Additional responsibilities include providing guidance and support to graduate assistants. We are seeking a highly organized, collaborative professional who demonstrates strong communication skills, attention to detail, initiative, and a commitment to supporting students and the work of the Occupational Therapy Department. Key Responsibilities • Supports clinical education faculty in each department • Communicate (in-person meeting, email, etc.) with students and clinical sites regarding clinical education contract, site and health requirements • Order/run state specific background checks as assigned • Track student records / data in applicable record management systems (i.e. EXXAT, other databases) • Prepare clinical education and field work site paperwork • Assist with event coordination for department, clinical and community partnerships • Support faculty with course management (LMS, Proctoring) • Use of office machines including photocopiers, telephones, FAX, computer and Microsoft Office products, etc • Monitor program, site, and contract requirement documentation • Maintain compliance and abide by FERPA Policy • Supports department and course organization of files • Supervises graduate assistants *ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: To perform this job successfully, the employee must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. A qualified individual is one who, with or without reasonable accommodations, can perform the essential functions of the position. GENERAL REQUIREMENTS A minimum of an associate's degree in a related area and knowledge of assigned work area. Strong written and verbal communication skills, detail oriented, accurate data entry and skill with computers including: working knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite, especially Word, Access and Excel, Internet capabilities, etc. Previous experience is preferred. Experience with health requirement compliance is a plus. Ability to professionally interact with all faculty, staff, students, and all constituents we serve. Occasionally it may be necessary to vary the number of hours and/or specific days worked per week. Compensation will be paid in accordance with the provisions of the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938, as amended. SPECIFIC REQUIREMENTS • Excellent interpersonal skills and professional behaviors. • Personal qualities of tact and diplomacy; ability to maintain a high level of confidentiality, to initiate • and conduct work with a minimum of supervision, and to accept constructive criticism. • Competent performance with a variety of office machines. • Competent performance in the use of Microsoft, Excel, Google. • Experience with medical terminology; understanding of common immunization/screening requirements, or willingness to learn. • Excellent organizational skills. • Willingness to learn new technology as needed. Benefits • Competitive salary commensurate with experience. • Comprehensive benefits package, including medical, dental, vision, and retirement plans. • Generous paid time off and holidays. • Tuition Remission. • Professional development opportunities. • Supportive and collaborative work environment. • Access to campus amenities, cultural events, and recreational facilities. St. Ambrose University and its affiliate campuses, including Mount Mercy University in Cedar Rapids, Iowa, are an inclusive, Catholic, and diocesan community of learners. Together, we empower all members of our community to act courageously, to seek wisdom through faith and reason, to work for justice and mercy, and to lead lives of service. St. Ambrose University and its affiliated institutions within its Catholic higher education network are equal opportunity employers. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit your resume, cover letter, and contact information for three professional references though our online application portal at https://stambroseuniv.applicantlist.com. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. #HEJ"
    }
  },
